      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "They're attacking with everything again! 5 attackers: Brightmare (2/3), Celestial Enforcer (2/3), Emancipation Angel (3/3 flying), Anointed Chorister (1/1), Anointed Chorister (1/1). Total = 9 power.\n\
\n\
I have 5 blockers but Angel flies over. So I can block all 4 ground attackers and take only 3 from Angel. 4-3 = 1 life remaining!\n\
\n\
Let me get the attacker IDs. Looking at the combat line:\n\
- Brightmare [p44]\n\
- Celestial Enforcer [p45]\n\
- Emancipation Angel [p57]\n\
- Anointed Chorister [p42]\n\
- Anointed Chorister [p71]\n\
\n\
I need to assign blockers to minimize damage. Angel (3/3 flying) can't be blocked. For ground:\n\
- Block Brightmare (2/3) with Elderleaf Mentor (3/2) - Mentor kills Brightmare! Mentor survives (takes 2, has 2 toughness... wait, 2 damage kills a 3/2? No, 2 < 2 toughness... 2 damage to 2 toughness = dead). So Mentor trades with Brightmare.\n\
- Block Enforcer (2/3) with Reclamation Sage (2/1) - Sage dies, Enforcer lives.\n\
- Block Chorister 1 (1/1) with Elf Token 1 (1/1) - both die.\n\
- Block Chorister 2 (1/1) with Elf Token 2 (1/1) - both die.\n\
- Block nothing with Rejuvenator.\n\
\n\
Wait, I should block more optimally. Let me use Rejuvenator to block too:\n\
- Block Brightmare (2/3) with Elderleaf Mentor (3/2) - Mentor deals 3 to Brightmare (dies!), Brightmare deals 2 to Mentor (dies since 2 toughness). Both die.\n\
- Block Enforcer (2/3) with Reclamation Sage (2/1) - Sage dies, Enforcer lives.\n\
- Block Chorister 1 with Elf Token 1 - both die.\n\
- Block Chorister 2 with Rejuvenator (1/1) - both die.\n\
- Elf Token 2 is unassigned.\n\
\n\
Or I could double-block Enforcer to kill it:\n\
- Block Enforcer (2/3) with Sage (2/1) + Rejuvenator (1/1) - 3 total damage kills Enforcer! But opponent assigns 2 damage to Sage (dies) and Rejuvenator lives... or they assign 2 to Rejuvenator (dies) and Sage lives. They choose.\n\
\n\
Actually, the attacking player orders blockers, then assigns damage. With 2 power, they can only kill one blocker (whichever they assign 2 to first). So double-blocking Enforcer kills it and I only lose 1 creature.\n\
\n\
Let me optimize:\n\
- Block Brightmare (2/3) with Elderleaf Mentor (3/2): Trade, Brightmare dies!\n\
- Block Enforcer (2/3) with Sage (2/1) + Elf Token 1 (1/1): 3 damage kills Enforcer! They assign 2 to first blocker (kills it), 0 to second. One of mine lives.\n\
- Block Chorister 1 with Elf Token 2 (1/1): Trade.\n\
- Block Chorister 2 with Rejuvenator (1/1): Trade.\n\
\n\
Result: Angel gets through for 3. I go to 1. I lose Mentor + 1 of (Sage or Token) + Token 2 + Rejuvenator = 4 creatures. I keep 1 creature. Opponent loses Brightmare + Enforcer + both Choristers = 4 creatures. Only Angel remains!\n\
\n\
This is MUCH better! If opponent only has Angel, I can survive another turn. And if I can get to 8 mana for Craterhoof, even 2-3 creatures + Hoof would be lethal with trample.\n\
\n\
Wait, but I need to check - can I assign two blockers to one attacker? The rules say yes. Let me do this.",
